      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To investigate the clinical effect of SR49059 when given shortly before the onset of menstruation as a preventative treatment of dysmenorrhoea."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "DESIGN",
          "content": "A double-blind, randomised, placebo-controlled, cross-over trial in complete block design (three periods, three treatments)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "SETTING",
          "content": "A clinical research organisation in Paris, France."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "PARTICIPANTS",
          "content": "Women aged 18-35 years suffering from primary dysmenorrhoea."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "INTERVENTIONS",
          "content": "In each of three menstrual cycles, women reported to the study centre and were given a daily dose of either placebo, 100 mg or 300 mg SR49059 from a minimum of 4 hours up to a maximum of three days before the onset of bleeding and/or menstrual pain. If this did not control the pain, women were allowed once a day to take a second dose of study treatment providing that at least 4 hours had passed since the first drug intake."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "MAIN OUTCOME MEASURES",
          "content": "Intensity of menstrual pain recorded by means of a visual analogue scale. Rating of symptoms of dysmenorrhoea (mainly back and pelvic pain) in relation to functional capacity (Sultan score). Self-assessment of menstrual blood loss in a menstrual diary record."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Analysis of intensity of menstrual pain, as recorded by visual analogue scale and Sultan pain score (back and pelvic pain) during the first 24 hours of dysmenorrhoea, showed a dose-related effect of SR49059. The 300 mg dose of SR49059 was significantly more effective than placebo. Similarly, a dose-related effect of SR49059 was shown on total Sultan score. SR49059 was well tolerated and no significant effect on the bleeding pattern was noted."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "This study showed for the first time a therapeutic effect of an orally active vasopressin V1a receptor antagonist in the prevention of dysmenorrhoea. Further studies are required to examine effect mechanisms and determine effective doses."
        }
      ]},
